{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a prince of the Crusader State County of Tripoli. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the sister of Melisende Queen of Jerusalem and the daughter of Baldwin II King of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Regency* \nRaymond III was only 12 years old when his father was assassinated in 1152. As a result he became the Count of Tripoli. Due to his young age a regent was appointed to govern the county until he came of age. The regent was his cousin Baldwin III King of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1173 after being held captive for nearly a decade. After his release he played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed as the regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy.\n\n*Reign and Military Campaigns* \nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and played a crucial role in several battles against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. He led a successful campaign against the forces of Saladin in 1175 and 1180. However he was unable to prevent the loss of the County of Tripoli to Saladin's forces in 1187.\n\n*Succession and Legacy* \nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir. The County of Tripoli was passed to his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy was marked by his military prowess and his role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was also known for his attempts to prevent the downfall of the Crusader States. He was a key figure in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin and the subsequent loss of Jerusalem to Saladin. Raymond III's death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Crusader